    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Preterm infants are at increased risk for developing neurodevelopmental disorders. One factor that can contribute to the development of these conditions is peri- and postnatal exposure to hypoxia, which is commonly sustained by premature infants because of their lung immaturity, hypotension, and poor cerebral blood flow regulation. To date, no effective treatments exist for brain injury of prematurity.The development of human brain organoids, or 3D tissue grown from human induced pluripotent stem cells, gives us a reliable yet noninvasive way to study neurodevelopment in a variety of conditions and at various developmental stages. Our lab’s development of a human cortical organoid (hCO) model of hypoxic brain injury of prematurity led to the finding of distinct transcriptional and cellular changes in hCOs after 24 and 48 hours in a low-oxygen environment and identified potential therapeutics.In both hCOs and patients, a period of reoxygenation follows hypoxia. Clinical studies have shown increased cell death following reoxygenation. We developed a novel human cellular model for reoxygenation using our hCO system to study the mechanisms of reoxygenation-induced brain injury and evaluate potential neuroprotective strategies.Using our model for reoxygenation, we consistently observed a significant increase in cell death during hypoxia and additional increase during reoxygenation. Next, we found that hypothermia is neuroprotective; this therapy is used in the hospital for term babies with neonatal hypoxic brain injuries but cannot be used in preterm infants due to high risk for complications. Lastly, we tested adrenomedullin, sodium iodide, and magnesium sulfate. While evaluation of these treatments requires additional validation, sodium iodide is a promising candidate for additional studies.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Gurwitz, E. and Pasca, A. (2024). A Model for Brain Reoxygenation-induced Injury Using Human Cortical Organoids. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/nq903gz7857. https://doi.org/10.25740/nq903gz7857.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
